body:
Predecessor: #3078
Related: #4833
Architecture: [ConduitOS protection domains](../blob/dev/docs/architecture/conduitos-protection-domains.md), [implementation confinement](../blob/dev/docs/architecture/implementation-confinement.md), device-protocol Root/Source boundary

## Outcome

Promote ConduitOS protection domains from the current x86_64 adversarial proof profile into the **ordinary execution realization for admitted execution regions**, and carry the same contract across every ConduitOS architecture backend.

The architecture-neutral law is:

> **Root owns privileged machine truth and physical authority. Ordinary admitted implementation execution runs in a protection domain that cannot reach Root, kernel state, sibling domains, or physical resources except through exact current capabilities.**

This is not “run Source in the kernel but check it carefully.” It is also not “Source may never execute.”

The intended split is:

```text
checked Source / admitted implementation
             │
             ▼
      execution region
             │
             ▼
   unprivileged protection domain
             │
        pure computation
        private state
        bounded queues
             │
             │ exact capability operation
             ▼
   trap / exception / call gate
             │
──────── privileged boundary ────────
             │
             ▼
            Root
   capability + lifecycle tables
   machine/resource ownership
   trusted Bases/providers
   interrupts / mandatory safety
             │
             ▼
       physical machine
```

#3078 established one real x86_64 CPL3/page-table/I/O-gate proof. This issue turns that mechanism into normal Plan/Play execution and defines equivalent realizations for the other ConduitOS architectures. One architecture's proof must not be generalized by analogy.

## Why now

#4833 sharpened the Root/Source boundary:

- Source owns portable protocol meaning.
- Plan selects a realization.
- Root independently owns the physical controller/resource/clock/authority.
- possession is issued only for the exact selected current Play.

Today that distinction is semantically strong, but most ordinary ConduitOS implementation code still executes with ambient kernel privilege. The processor therefore does not yet enforce the same boundary that the architecture describes.

The existing x86_64 protection-domain work shows that ConduitOS already has the critical pieces:

- unprivileged execution;
- domain-local address spaces;
- a privileged trap stack;
- an architecture-neutral kernel capability table;
- exact Host/Boot/Plan/Play/Base/resource/operation binding;
- refusal of arbitrary, stolen, stale, broadened, and exhausted handles;
- processor-enforced denial of kernel memory, sibling memory, MMIO, direct I/O, and arbitrary kernel entry.

The missing step is to make this an **ordinary execution path**, rather than a special `conduitos-isolation-proof` fixture.

## Critical distinction: Source is allowed to execute

Do not interpret confinement as “portable Source is inert forever and Root performs all work.”

Source must remain capable of doing substantial local computation:

- arithmetic;
- parsing and binary composition;
- state machines;
- protocol progression;
- filtering/transformation;
- bounded retained state;
- ordinary scheduling/flow;
- pure semantic computation.

What Source must not do is turn its own descriptions into ambient physical authority.

A protection domain should therefore execute the admitted computation itself. It may use:

- lowered native code;
- a small Conduit execution engine;
- AOT-produced code;
- another reviewed bounded implementation format;

provided the resulting implementation runs outside privileged Root and has only the memory/capabilities actually admitted.

The dangerous anti-goal is:

```text
Source
  ↓
"request Root to execute every semantic step for me"
```

That would collapse Conduit into a privileged interpreter/RPC surface and put most execution back inside the trusted computing base.

The target is instead:

```text
Source computation runs here
        │
        │ only when an admitted effect is required
        ▼
small Root capability gate
```

## Protection-domain unit

Use the existing **execution region** as the primary realization boundary.

Do not invent process-per-Gear as a universal rule.

An admitted Plan fragment / execution region should be realizable as one protection domain when its trust and authority envelope permit it. Several pure gears may share that domain. Separate domains remain possible where authority, fault containment, lifecycle, or hostile implementation boundaries require them.

Keep distinct:

```text
Body identity
Host / Boot
Plan / Play
Plan fragment
execution region
protection-domain identity
implementation identity
kernel capability handles
Base/resource identity and generation
physical resource
```

Protection-domain identity is realization/security truth, not authored semantic identity.

## Architecture-neutral domain contract

Introduce or consolidate a small target-neutral ConduitOS protection-domain interface around responsibilities equivalent to:

- create/bind one domain to current Host/Boot/Plan/Play/execution region;
- install executable read/execute memory;
- install bounded private mutable state;
- install bounded stack(s);
- expose only explicitly shared bounded queue/input/output pages or copy windows;
- enter unprivileged execution;
- resume after a permitted gate;
- classify and retain faults;
- revoke all handles on lifecycle/resource replacement;
- destroy/quarantine the domain without leaking authority;
- never expose raw kernel/device pointers.

The portable execution/kernel layers should not know about x86 rings, AArch64 ELs, RISC-V privilege names, or LoongArch PLVs.

## Architecture realizations

### x86_64

Promote the existing #3078 mechanism into ordinary execution:

- Root/kernel at CPL0;
- implementation domain at CPL3;
- per-domain user mappings;
- TSS ring-0 trap stack;
- user-invocable narrow gate;
- no user-accessible kernel/device/capability-table mappings;
- direct I/O denied;
- capability validation before Base effect.

The ordinary graphical/product path must exercise this path, not only `conduitos-isolation-proof`.

### IA-32

Implement the same contract with the appropriate 32-bit protected-mode mechanism:

- ring 0 / ring 3;
- page-table user/supervisor separation;
- kernel trap stack/TSS as required;
- narrow trap/syscall gate;
- direct privileged/I/O/device access denied.

Do not claim IA-32 parity from the x86_64 proof.

### AArch64

Realize the same boundary using the architecture's normal privilege split:

- Root/kernel in EL1;
- implementation execution in EL0;
- stage-1 translation and EL0 permissions;
- exception entry through a narrow SVC/exception gate;
- no EL0 mapping of kernel/device/capability-table state;
- Base effects only after current capability validation.

If a board/boot environment enters at another EL, normalize that boot fact without changing the semantic contract.

### RISC-V64

Realize the boundary using:

- Root/kernel supervisor privilege;
- implementation execution in U-mode;
- page-table permissions appropriate to the target profile;
- `ecall`/trap gate;
- no direct supervisor/device state access;
- exact capability table validation in the privileged handler.

Do not confuse firmware/SBI service availability with Conduit capability authority.

### LoongArch64

Realize the boundary using the architecture's user/kernel privilege levels and page protections:

- privileged Root/kernel;
- least-privileged implementation execution;
- exception/syscall gate;
- no user mapping of Root/device/capability state;
- exact Base capability validation on entry.

Use LoongArch terminology in the implementation/proof, but keep it out of the universal contract.

### ARMv6 / additional ConduitOS architecture backends

The source tree also contains an ARMv6 architecture backend. If it remains a supported ConduitOS execution backend, give it an explicit protection-domain disposition using its user/privileged modes and MMU where honestly available.

If a target/profile cannot provide the required hardware-enforced boundary, it must report **protected ordinary execution unsupported**. It must not silently run the same untrusted implementation at full privilege while claiming equivalent confinement.

The same rule applies to future architecture backends.

## Root contract

Root remains deliberately small and privileged.

It may own:

- boot normalization;
- page-table/domain construction;
- exception/interrupt entry;
- kernel capability and lifecycle tables;
- physical memory/resource ownership;
- provider/resource generations;
- trusted low-level Bases/drivers;
- mandatory local safety;
- capability issuance/revocation;
- bounded copy/shared-memory validation at the domain boundary.

Root must not absorb ordinary semantic execution merely because a domain needs an effect.

Device protocol meaning, parsers, state machines, ordinary policy, calibration math, transformations, and other portable computation remain above Root.

## Capability gate

Reuse the architecture-neutral capability semantics already proven by #3078 rather than inventing an architecture-specific syscall API.

A domain operation must be authorized against exact current facts including:

```text
protection domain
Host / Boot
Plan / Play
implementation
Base + generation
resource + generation
operation
subject / authority
parameter bound
work bound
in-flight bound
operation count
revocation generation
```

The caller presents only opaque domain-local capability material and bounded parameters.

The privileged gate must reject at least:

- unknown handle;
- another domain's handle;
- stale/revoked handle;
- wrong operation;
- wrong Play/Plan/Boot;
- wrong Base/resource generation;
- excessive bytes/work;
- excess operations/in-flight work;
- malformed user pointer/range;
- user buffer crossing unmapped or unauthorized memory.

A successful trap is not itself semantic completion. Completion remains ordinary Play/kernel truth.

## Pure computation path

Pure execution should be cheap.

A pure execution region needs no Base capability gate for each semantic operation. Once entered, it should be able to progress bounded computation in its own domain until it:

- reaches an effect boundary;
- needs admitted external input;
- exhausts work;
- yields under scheduler policy;
- completes;
- faults;
- is cancelled/revoked.

This is crucial. The protection architecture must not turn every arithmetic node, Cord movement, or local state transition into a supervisor round trip.

## Effect path and batching

A Base operation necessarily crosses the privileged boundary, but do not assume one trap per byte, sample, pixel, packet field, or semantic micro-step.

Permit reviewed bounded mechanisms such as:

- batched Base operations;
- fixed shared request/completion rings;
- bounded shared pages with ownership transitions;
- copy-in/copy-out for small requests;
- interrupt-driven completion with exact domain wake;
- amortized queue drains;

while preserving the same exact capability envelope.

Optimization may reduce transitions. It may not introduce ambient shared authority.

## Memory model

A domain must not be able to address:

- Root/kernel text or mutable state;
- kernel capability tables;
- sibling domain private state;
- sibling stacks;
- arbitrary physical memory;
- device MMIO;
- DMA control structures;
- boot modules as authority-bearing state.

Map only what the admitted execution requires.

Code pages should be non-writable once admitted. Mutable state and stacks should be non-executable where architecture support permits it.

Explicitly classify shared queue pages and any executable runtime support rather than relying on inherited kernel mappings.

## Fault and lifecycle behavior

A processor fault from untrusted implementation code is a domain/execution refusal, not a whole-machine semantic success or silent kernel panic.

Retain exact fault/refusal truth and revoke the domain's outstanding capabilities on:

- Play cancellation/completion;
- Plan replacement;
- authority revocation;
- Base/resource replacement;
- provider loss;
- Boot replacement;
- terminal protection fault.

Where possible, an unrelated sibling domain/Play should remain intact.

Do not promise general fault containment until the scheduler/product path actually supports it.

## Scheduling

Keep scheduling separate from privilege.

Dropping to user mode does not require a Unix process scheduler. The current cooperative Conduit kernel may still choose when a bounded domain runs.

However, a hostile or buggy user domain must not gain infinite machine ownership merely because it cannot touch privileged memory. The design must state how bounded work, timer/preemption/yield, cancellation, or another reviewed mechanism returns control to Root.

An implementation that executes an infinite pure loop is still a denial of service unless Root can regain the CPU.

## DMA and provider caveat

CPU privilege/page isolation does not isolate a trusted kernel driver that programs malicious DMA, nor does it constrain a hostile physical device without an IOMMU/bounce-buffer/other mechanism.

Preserve the current truthful distinction:

- implementation/domain isolation;
- driver/provider isolation;
- DMA isolation.

Do not claim the latter two merely because ordinary Source now runs unprivileged.

## Same Source across targets

One acceptance specimen should use the **same exact checked Source and semantic Plan shape** across the supported ConduitOS architecture realizations.

Target code may differ in:

- page-table format;
- privilege transition;
- exception frame;
- trap instruction;
- TLB maintenance;
- interrupt wiring;
- ABI register placement.

Source must not contain any of those target facts.

## Product integration

This issue is not complete if every architecture has only an isolated synthetic privilege-switch demo.

At least one ordinary body/plot execution path must use protection domains as its real product execution mechanism, and each supported architecture backend must exercise the same protection contract through its normal product/proof lane.

The existing x86_64 adversarial fixture should remain useful as a negative test, but it must no longer be the only place where CPL3 exists.

## Performance and cost evidence

Measure rather than hand-wave the cost introduced by protection:

- privilege/exception transitions per Play;
- Base-gate transitions per operation;
- bytes copied across the gate;
- shared-page/ring occupancy;
- page-table/TLB switches;
- domain setup/teardown work;
- memory reserved per domain;
- scheduler returns/preemptions.

Do not set a universal magic threshold in advance. Use the measurements to identify pathological designs such as one trap per tiny semantic step and to justify batching/shared-ring work.

A correctness path may be slower first. The architecture must nevertheless preserve a route to useful throughput without relaxing authority.

## Suggested implementation sequence

1. Extract the existing x86_64 proof mechanism behind an architecture-neutral protection-domain seam.
2. Run one ordinary pure execution region at x86_64 CPL3.
3. Run one ordinary effectful region at CPL3 with one existing Base capability.
4. Ensure the region performs substantial pure computation without Root calls.
5. Route Base effects through the exact capability gate.
6. Integrate cancellation, completion and revocation with the ordinary Body/Play lifecycle.
7. Promote the path into the ordinary x86_64 product.
8. Implement IA-32 realization.
9. Implement AArch64 EL0 realization.
10. Implement RISC-V64 U-mode realization.
11. Implement LoongArch64 least-privilege realization.
12. Give ARMv6/other current backends an honest implementation or explicit unsupported disposition.
13. Add cross-architecture conformance using the same Source.
14. Retain target-specific negative privilege/memory/device attempts.
15. Publish cost/transition measurements and optimize pathological crossings without weakening the contract.
16. Update STATUS and architecture docs with exact per-target claims.

## Acceptance criteria

### Universal

- [ ] protection domain is an architecture-neutral realization beneath ordinary execution regions;
- [ ] Source/semantic code can perform substantial pure computation inside the unprivileged domain;
- [ ] ordinary semantic execution is not delegated step-by-step back into Root;
- [ ] effectful operations require exact current Root-held capability admission;
- [ ] kernel/Root/capability tables/device resources are inaccessible to the domain except through admitted gates;
- [ ] stale/stolen/arbitrary/broadened capability use is refused;
- [ ] lifecycle replacement/revocation invalidates domain capabilities;
- [ ] hostile non-effectful looping has a documented bounded return/preemption mechanism;
- [ ] same Source contains no target privilege/page-table facts;
- [ ] cost measurements exist for crossings, copying and domain memory.

### x86_64

- [ ] ordinary product execution uses CPL3 protection domains, not only the #3078 proof feature;
- [ ] existing page/I/O/capability negative proof remains valid.

### IA-32

- [ ] ordinary protected execution runs outside ring 0 with page and I/O privilege enforcement.

### AArch64

- [ ] ordinary protected execution runs at EL0 under privileged Root/kernel control.

### RISC-V64

- [ ] ordinary protected execution runs in U-mode under the ConduitOS privileged kernel.

### LoongArch64

- [ ] ordinary protected execution runs at the architecture's least-privileged application level with exact exception-gate enforcement.

### ARMv6 / other backend

- [ ] current supported backend either implements the same protection contract or explicitly refuses protected execution without claiming parity.

## Stop line

Do not close this issue with:

- one synthetic privilege-switch fixture per CPU but ordinary Plays still in privileged mode;
- a Source model that cannot actually perform local computation;
- “everything is a syscall” semantic execution;
- one trap per trivial arithmetic/Cord/state step;
- raw kernel/device pointers mapped into untrusted domains;
- writable capability tables in domain memory;
- architecture-specific privilege facts in Source or Plan semantics;
- ring-0 fallback presented as protected execution;
- x86_64 evidence presented as proof for IA-32/AArch64/RISC-V64/LoongArch64;
- CPU isolation presented as DMA or driver isolation;
- a Unix process model added merely because the hardware has user mode.

## Done means

An ordinary ConduitOS Plan can place an execution region on any supported architecture and truthfully mean:

> **The implementation itself runs here. Root does not run its ordinary semantics for it. The processor prevents it from reaching anything outside its admitted domain, and the only path to physical authority is the exact capability the current Play was given.**
